Use one JSON file, loaded fully into memory and rewritten on every change.

**Location:** `filepath.Join(os.UserConfigDir(), "books", "books.json")`  
On Linux: `~/.config/books/books.json`, macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/books/books.json`, Windows: `%AppData%\books\books.json`.

**Shape:**

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "books": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "The Go Programming Language",
      "author": "Donovan & Kernighan",
      "status": "reading",
      "page": 42,
      "created_at": "2026-05-07T12:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}
```

Why an object instead of a bare array? You can add fields later without breaking the file.

**Save atomically:**

```go
func save(path string, v any) error {
    b, err := json.MarshalIndent(v, "", "  ")
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }

    dir := filepath.Dir(path)
    tmp, err := os.CreateTemp(dir, "books-*.tmp")
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    defer os.Remove(tmp.Name())

    if err := tmp.Chmod(0600); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if _, err := tmp.Write(b); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if err := tmp.Sync(); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if err := tmp.Close(); err != nil {
        return err
    }

    return os.Rename(tmp.Name(), path)
}
```

**Rules:**

- Missing file = empty list.
- Create parent dir with `0700`.
- Write file with `0600`.
- Never truncate/overwrite the real file directly.
- Load once, mutate in memory, save once per command.
- Don’t add locking yet. If you later run multiple instances or store thousands of books, switch to SQLite.

Dispatch on `os.Args[1]`, then use a `flag.FlagSet` per subcommand.

```go
func main() {
	if len(os.Args) < 2 {
		fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "usage: books <add|list|update|remove>")
		os.Exit(1)
	}

	var err error
	switch os.Args[1] {
	case "add":
		err = cmdAdd(os.Args[2:])
	default:
		err = fmt.Errorf("unknown command %q", os.Args[1])
	}
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "error:", err)
		os.Exit(1)
	}
}

func cmdAdd(args []string) error {
	fs := flag.NewFlagSet("add", flag.ExitOnError)
	author := fs.String("author", "", "book author")
	if err := fs.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return err
	}
	title := fs.Arg(0)
	if title == "" {
		return errors.New("add: title required")
	}

	path, err := dataPath()
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	store, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}

	var maxID int64
	for _, b := range store.Books {
		if b.ID > maxID {
			maxID = b.ID
		}
	}

	store.Books = append(store.Books, Book{
		ID:        maxID + 1,
		Title:     title,
		Author:    *author,
		Status:    StatusReading,
		Page:      0,
		CreatedAt: time.Now().UTC(),
	})

	return save(path, store)
}
```

Usage:

```
books add "The Go Programming Language" --author "Donovan & Kernighan"
```

The flag package requires flags before positional args, so `--author` must come before the title, or switch to `fs.Parse` on reordered args. Simplest fix: quote the title last as shown.

Allow them. Titles aren’t unique — same title can be different authors, editions, translations, or a reread. Your `ID` is the unique key.

If you want to catch accidental exact duplicates, check `title + author` (trimmed, case-insensitive) before appending:

```go
for _, b := range store.Books {
    if strings.EqualFold(strings.TrimSpace(b.Title), strings.TrimSpace(title)) &&
       strings.EqualFold(strings.TrimSpace(b.Author), strings.TrimSpace(*author)) {
        return fmt.Errorf("already have %q by %s (id %d)", b.Title, b.Author, b.ID)
    }
}
```

But don’t make `title` a key. If you later want to allow forced duplicates, add a `--force` flag.

```go
type Store struct {
	Version int    `json:"version"`
	Books   []Book `json:"books"`
}

func dataPath() (string, error) {
	dir, err := os.UserConfigDir()
	if err != nil {
		return "", err
	}
	return filepath.Join(dir, "books", "books.json"), nil
}

func load(path string) (*Store, error) {
	b, err := os.ReadFile(path)
	if errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {
		return &Store{Version: 1, Books: []Book{}}, nil
	}
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	var s Store
	if err := json.Unmarshal(b, &s); err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("parse %s: %w", path, err)
	}
	if s.Books == nil {
		s.Books = []Book{}
	}
	return &s, nil
}
```

Notes:

- Missing file = fresh store with an empty slice, so `list` doesn’t blow up on a first run.
- `s.Books = []Book{}` makes JSON marshal back to `"books": []` instead of `null`.
- Corrupt file returns an error naming the path; don’t silently reset, or you’ll delete the user’s list.
- Parent dir is created by `save`, so `load` doesn’t need to.

```go
func save(path string, s *Store) error {
	b, err := json.MarshalIndent(s, "", "  ")
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	b = append(b, '\n')

	dir := filepath.Dir(path)
	if err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0700); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	tmp, err := os.CreateTemp(dir, "books-*.tmp")
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	tmpName := tmp.Name()
	defer os.Remove(tmpName) // no-op after successful rename

	if err := tmp.Chmod(0600); err != nil {
		tmp.Close()
		return err
	}
	if _, err := tmp.Write(b); err != nil {
		tmp.Close()
		return err
	}
	if err := tmp.Sync(); err != nil {
		tmp.Close()
		return err
	}
	if err := tmp.Close(); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	return os.Rename(tmpName, path)
}
```

Why it’s shaped this way:

- **Temp file in the same dir** — `os.Rename` is atomic only within one filesystem.
- **`Sync` before `Close`** — the bytes hit disk before the rename, so a crash can’t leave a renamed-but-empty file.
- **`defer os.Remove`** — cleans up on any early return; after a successful rename it just fails silently.
- **`MkdirAll` first** — first-run `add` needs the parent dir to exist.
- **`0600`/`0700`** — wins with `CreateTemp`’s default 0600 anyway, but explicit.

Call it as `save(path, store)` from each mutating command. Read-only commands (`list`) never call `save`.

Add an `update` subcommand, or a `finish` shortcut that calls it.

```go
func cmdUpdate(args []string) error {
	fs := flag.NewFlagSet("update", flag.ExitOnError)
	status := fs.String("status", "", "reading|finished")
	page := fs.Int("page", -1, "current page")
	if err := fs.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	id, err := strconv.ParseInt(fs.Arg(0), 10, 64)
	if err != nil {
		return errors.New("update: id required")
	}

	path, err := dataPath()
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	store, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}

	var book *Book
	for i := range store.Books {
		if store.Books[i].ID == id {
			book = &store.Books[i]
			break
		}
	}
	if book == nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("no book with id %d", id)
	}

	if *status != "" {
		switch Status(*status) {
		case StatusReading, StatusFinished:
			book.Status = Status(*status)
		default:
			return fmt.Errorf("invalid status %q", *status)
		}
	}
	if *page >= 0 {
		book.Page = *page
	}

	return save(path, store)
}
```

Usage:

```
books update --status finished 3
books update --status finished --page 210 3
```

Put flags **before** the id — Go’s `flag` package stops parsing at the first non-flag argument.

If you want a shorter command:

```go
case "finish":
	err = cmdUpdate(append([]string{"--status", "finished"}, os.Args[2:]...))
```

Then:

```
books finish 3
```

`list` should print the ID so you know which number to use:

```
3  The Go Programming Language  Donovan & Kernighan  finished  p.210
```

Add a `--status` filter to `list` rather than a whole new command.

```go
func cmdList(args []string) error {
	fs := flag.NewFlagSet("list", flag.ExitOnError)
	status := fs.String("status", "", "reading|finished")
	if err := fs.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	path, err := dataPath()
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	store, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}

	for _, b := range store.Books {
		if *status != "" && string(b.Status) != *status {
			continue
		}
		fmt.Printf("%d  %s  %s  %s  p.%d\n", b.ID, b.Title, b.Author, b.Status, b.Page)
	}
	return nil
}
```

Usage:

```
books list --status reading
books list --status finished
```

Same flag-before-args rule as before, but here there are no positional args, so it’s always safe.

If you’d rather have a dedicated verb:

```go
case "unfinished":
	err = cmdList([]string{"--status", "reading"})
```

then `books unfinished` works too. For a personal tool I’d keep the filter and skip the alias until you catch yourself typing it.

Test `load`/`save` as plain functions — they already take a `path`, so never call `dataPath()` in tests. Use `t.TempDir()`, which is cleaned up automatically.

Cases worth covering:

- missing file → empty store, no error
- round trip: `save` then `load` returns the same data
- `save` creates the parent directory
- corrupt JSON → error that names the path
- `save` leaves no temp files behind

```go
package main

import (
	"os"
	"path/filepath"
	"reflect"
	"strings"
	"testing"
	"time"
)

func sample() *Store {
	return &Store{Version: 1, Books: []Book{{
		ID:        1,
		Title:     "The Go Programming Language",
		Author:    "Donovan & Kernighan",
		Status:    StatusReading,
		Page:      42,
		CreatedAt: time.Unix(0, 0).UTC(),
	}}}
}

func TestLoadMissingFile(t *testing.T) {
	path := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), "books.json")

	s, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("load: %v", err)
	}
	if len(s.Books) != 0 {
		t.Fatalf("want empty store, got %d books", len(s.Books))
	}
}

func TestSaveThenLoad(t *testing.T) {
	// parent dir intentionally doesn't exist yet
	path := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), "books", "books.json")
	want := sample()

	if err := save(path, want); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("save: %v", err)
	}
	got, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("load: %v", err)
	}
	if !reflect.DeepEqual(got, want) {
		t.Errorf("round trip mismatch:\n got %+v\nwant %+v", got, want)
	}
}

func TestLoadCorrupt(t *testing.T) {
	path := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), "books.json")
	if err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte("{ not json"), 0600); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}

	_, err := load(path)
	if err == nil {
		t.Fatal("want error for corrupt file")
	}
	if !strings.Contains(err.Error(), path) {
		t.Errorf("error should name the path, got: %v", err)
	}
}

func TestSaveLeavesNoTempFiles(t *testing.T) {
	dir := t.TempDir()
	if err := save(filepath.Join(dir, "books.json"), sample()); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}

	entries, err := os.ReadDir(dir)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if len(entries) != 1 || entries[0].Name() != "books.json" {
		t.Fatalf("unexpected dir contents: %v", entries)
	}
}
```

Notes:

- **`time.Time` and `reflect.DeepEqual`** — the sample uses `time.Unix(0,0).UTC()` because it has no monotonic reading. If you build times with `time.Now()`, DeepEqual will fail after a round trip even though the values are "the same." Compare with `.Equal()` instead.
- **Corrupt test asserts the path appears in the error.** That's the contract that keeps `load` from silently resetting a damaged list.
- **Don't test** exact JSON formatting, temp-file names, or that `os.Rename` was called. Those are implementation details you're allowed to change.
- **Crash-safety is not unit-testable** without fault injection. The temp-file + rename structure is what you're relying on; verify it by review, not by test.

Running: `go test ./...`. Tests are milliseconds because `t.TempDir()` is on a real filesystem and the fixtures are tiny.

The round trip is the one that matters most — it's the contract every command depends on.

```go
package main

import (
	"path/filepath"
	"reflect"
	"testing"
	"time"
)

func TestSaveThenLoad(t *testing.T) {
	// Parent dir doesn't exist yet, so this also covers MkdirAll.
	path := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), "books", "books.json")

	want := &Store{Version: 1, Books: []Book{{
		ID:        1,
		Title:     "The Go Programming Language",
		Author:    "Donovan & Kernighan",
		Status:    StatusReading,
		Page:      42,
		CreatedAt: time.Unix(0, 0).UTC(),
	}}}

	if err := save(path, want); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("save: %v", err)
	}

	got, err := load(path)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("load: %v", err)
	}
	if !reflect.DeepEqual(got, want) {
		t.Errorf("round trip mismatch:\n got %+v\nwant %+v", got, want)
	}
}
```

Two things doing quiet work here:

- `t.TempDir()` gives a real directory and deletes it when the test ends — no manual cleanup.
- `time.Unix(0, 0).UTC()` instead of `time.Now()`: `time.Now()` carries a monotonic clock reading that JSON doesn't preserve, so `reflect.DeepEqual` would fail on a value that's actually equal.

Run it with `go test -run TestSaveThenLoad ./...`.

Add a `--sort` flag to `list`, defaulting to insertion order so nothing changes unless you ask.

```go
func cmdList(args []string) error {
	fs := flag.NewFlagSet("list", flag.ExitOnError)
	status := fs.String("status", "", "reading|finished")
	sortBy := fs.String("sort", "", "created")
	if err := fs.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	// ... load store ...

	if *sortBy == "created" {
		sort.SliceStable(store.Books, func(i, j int) bool {
			return store.Books[i].CreatedAt.Before(store.Books[j].CreatedAt)
		})
	}

	for _, b := range store.Books {
		// ... print ...
	}
	return nil
}
```

Usage:

```
books list --sort created
```

Notes:

- **`SliceStable`** — books added in the same second keep their insertion order instead of shuffling. `Slice` may reorder equals.
- **Sort a copy if you re-save.** Right now `cmdList` never calls `save`, so sorting `store.Books` in place is safe. If you later add a command that sorts *and* saves, sort a local slice or you'll rewrite the file on every `list`.
- **Don't store the sort order.** Recompute it each run; it's cheap and avoids a flag that can get out of sync with the data.
- **`CreatedAt` can be zero** for books written before you added the field. `time.Time{}.Before(any)` is true, so they'd float to the top. If that matters, fall back to `ID`:

```go
if store.Books[i].CreatedAt.Equal(store.Books[j].CreatedAt) {
	return store.Books[i].ID < store.Books[j].ID
}
```

For newest-first, flip the comparison — but keep that as a separate `--reverse` flag rather than another value of `--sort`.
